  <p class="standfirst">For most of the internet's history we built websites for two audiences: people and search engines. There's now a third, and it changes what your website has to do.</p>

  <section class="tldr" aria-label="Summary">
    <h2>TL;DR</h2>
    <p>Your website has a new audience. AI systems are increasingly involved in how people research products, compare alternatives, answer buying questions, and discover brands. This doesn't mean redesigning your site for robots. It means the information surrounding your brand needs to become clearer and more complete.</p>
    <p>An AI system trying to understand your company may need answers to questions like:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>What do you sell, and who is it for?</li>
      <li>What problem does it solve, and when would someone use it?</li>
      <li>How is it different, and what does it cost?</li>
      <li>Does it fit this specific situation, and how does it compare with alternatives?</li>
      <li>What evidence supports your claims, and what do customers commonly ask?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Those happen to be many of the same questions your customers have. That's the opportunity. Build a website that's exceptionally useful to humans and exceptionally easy for machines to understand.</p>
    <p><strong>The easier your brand is to understand, the easier it is to recommend.</strong></p>
  </section>

